GetFieldRes addHashFieldToReply(client *c, kvobj *o, sds field, int hfeFlags) {
    if (o == NULL) {
        addReplyNull(c);
        return GETF_NOT_FOUND;
    }

    unsigned char *vstr = NULL;
    unsigned int vlen = UINT_MAX;
    long long vll = LLONG_MAX;

    GetFieldRes res = hashTypeGetValue(c->db, o, field, &vstr, &vlen, &vll, hfeFlags, NULL);
    if (res == GETF_OK) {
        if (vstr) {
            addReplyBulkCBuffer(c, vstr, vlen);
        } else {
            addReplyBulkLongLong(c, vll);
        }
    } else {
        addReplyNull(c);
    }
    return res;
}

char buf[MAX_LONG_DOUBLE_CHARS];
int len = ld2string(buf,sizeof(buf),value,LD_STR_HUMAN);
new = sdsnewlen(buf,len);
hashTypeSet(c->db, o,c->argv[2]->ptr,new,HASH_SET_TAKE_VALUE);
addReplyBulkCBuffer(c,buf,len);

robj *newobj;
newobj = createRawStringObject(buf,len);
rewriteClientCommandVector(c, 6, shared.hsetex, c->argv[1], shared.keepttl,
                    shared.fields, shared.integers[1], c->argv[2], newobj);
decrRefCount(newobj);

findings.body:
The rewrite passes **seven** arguments (`HSETEX`, key, `KEEPTTL`, `FIELDS`, `1`, field, value) but declares `argc = 6`:

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2569-2573"}

`rewriteClientCommandVector` (src/networking.c:4158) reads exactly `argc` varargs with `va_arg`, so `newobj` is never read. What gets propagated is:

```
HSETEX h1 KEEPTTL FIELDS 1 f1
```

That still passes HSETEX's arity check (`-6`), but parsing `FIELDS 1` expects a field/value pair, so the command fails on the replica and on AOF load. Every HINCRBYFLOAT therefore fails to apply on replicas. The replica keeps the stale value, and an AOF restart either errors or loses the increment.

::callout{type="warning"}
This is worse than the original bug: before, only the TTL diverged; now the value itself diverges.

The test's stream pattern `{hsetex h1 KEEPTTL FIELDS 1 f1 *}` needs a trailing space plus value, so it should fail against this code. Don't relax that pattern to make it pass.

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2571-2572"}
    rewriteClientCommandVector(c, 7, shared.hsetex, c->argv[1], shared.keepttl,
                        shared.fields, shared.integers[1], c->argv[2], newobj);

This PR is about the *replica* losing the TTL, but it also changes the master's local write:

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2559-2559"}

Without `HASH_SET_KEEP_TTL`, `hashTypeSet` removes the existing field expiration (see the `!(flags & HASH_SET_KEEP_TTL)` branch near line 989). Once the argc bug is fixed, the replica runs `HSETEX ... KEEPTTL` and **keeps** the TTL, while the master **drops** it. That is the same master/replica inconsistency the PR is fixing, just in the other direction. It also breaks the 7.4+ behavior that HINCRBYFLOAT keeps the field TTL on the master, and it no longer matches `hincrbyCommand` at line 2506, which still passes `HASH_SET_KEEP_TTL`.

The new test only runs `httl` against the replica (`r`), never the master (`r -1`), so it can't catch this.

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2559-2559"}
    hashTypeSet(c->db, o,c->argv[2]->ptr,new,HASH_SET_TAKE_VALUE | HASH_SET_KEEP_TTL);

This change has nothing to do with the fix:

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2576-2576"}

No header declares `addHashFieldToReply` (or `GetFieldRes`), and its only callers are in t_hash.c. Exporting it adds a global symbol and can trigger `-Wmissing-prototypes` warnings, for no benefit. Please revert it, or add a proper declaration in server.h if another file is going to use it.
